<a name="getting-started"></a>
# rez2ans — Getting Started (Main User Docs) 😎💾

Welcome, scene traveler. rez2ans is a GUI tool for turning your PNG/JPG into 80xN CP437 goodness — exporting either:

- ANSI: .ans / .ansi (BBS-ready)
- TheDraw/PabloDraw BIN: .bin (classic editor format)

Think of it as: open image → drag crop → render → save → post on the board like a legend.

Heads-up: the build you've got literally identifies itself as "rez2ans v .01 Alpha". So yeah… it's a spicy alpha. Expect rough edges, but also vibes.

---

## Quickstart (the "get on the wire NOW" path)

1) File → Open Image…
2) Drag a selection box on the image (this defines your 80xN crop target).
3) Pick your Mode, tweak the Gamma / Contrast / Saturation / Dither knobs.
4) Smash F12 (Render).
5) Save… (or Alt+S) → pick ANSI (.ans/.ansi) or BIN (.bin).

If you forget step 2, the app will basically bark: "Open an image, drag a box to crop, then Render."

---

## Keyboard shortcuts (because real ones don't click menus)

F12  — Render current mode (main "make it happen" key)  
Alt+S — Save (ANSI/BIN)  
Alt+R — RESET EVERYTHING to factory defaults (panic button)

There's also a legacy Tronicshade render path mentioned as F11 in the codebase, but your day-to-day muscle memory is F12.

---

## The Layout (what you're looking at)

At a high level you've got:

- Left: Source image + your selection box (crop)
- Right: Preview of the ANSI/BIN render (your future fame)
- Controls for:
  - Mode
  - Palette
  - Glyph set
  - Dithering
  - Image tuning (gamma/contrast/etc)
  - Shaders / patchbooks / Tronicshade (advanced wizardry)

You'll also see status text like:
- Selection: (x,y) - (x,y) size=... px
- Loaded: ... | Output: 80 x N | BIN bytes: ...

---

## Modes (choose your flavor of crunchy)

rez2ans ships with a bunch of render modes:

- hires — sharper detail, more "pixel decisions"
- shades — shade/block driven, classic chunky ANSI feel
- hybrid — mix of hires + shades (often a sweet spot)
- cartoon — bolder simplification (good for line art / flat art)
- colorbook — more stylized color treatment
- glyphfit — tries harder to "fit" characters to shapes/texture
- autoshader — leans into shader-assisted styling
- tronicshade — a texture/style pass that can feel "dither-like" but character-driven

Practical advice (scene-tested energy):
- Photos → start with hybrid
- Line art → try cartoon or shades
- Pixel art → jump to ANSI Art Mode (see below)
- Want stylized grit → tronicshade / autoshader

---

## ANSI Art Mode (pixel art enjoyers, assemble)

There's a dedicated ANSI Art Mode aimed at ANSI-ish glyph workflows like:

- ansiblocks
- ansiblocks-pixel
- "Load ANSI…" support (bring in an existing .ans / .ansi)

Also includes Glyph-only options:
- Glyph-only (characters only)
- Glyph-only (keep existing colors; change characters only)

That's super handy if you've already got a palette pass you like and just want to re-char the piece.

---

## "Color Hints" (teach it your palette like a wise sysop)

Hints bias palette matching so pastel/saturated colors don't wash out.

### The hint workflow
1) Drag a selection on the image (left panel)
2) Choose the ANSI color index you want it to be
3) Click "Add hint from selection"

There's also a Dropper mode:
- Turn it on, then click the image preview to sample + create a hint.

### Hint knobs you'll see
- Hint tolerance (0..255)
  How close a pixel has to be before it counts as "this hinted color".
- Hint strength
  How hard it pushes palette matching toward that ANSI color.
- Options like:
  - Use hints to build a temporary palette (VGAPAL)
  - Refit hinted palette each pass (gentle)
  - Post-fix colors using hints (final pass) (snaps FG/BG to hinted index if very close)

When to use hints:
- Skin tones turning into swamp green
- Pastels getting murdered by the nearest ANSI neighbor
- Logos needing exact brand-ish colors in the 16-color prison

---

## ANSIrez Mode (cleanup pass for less speckle, more "finished")

ANSIrez Mode applies a post-quantization smoothing pass:

- Filters: median, blend, brite, 4x4
- Useful for: reducing pepper-noise, thickening thin lines a bit
- Tradeoff: may soften detail (worth it often)

Suggested starting knobs:
- Brightness: 0.90–1.10
- Gamma:      0.95–1.05
- Contrast:   1.10–1.25
- Saturation: 0.90–1.10

If your render looks like it got faxed through a toaster, back off contrast and try median.

---

## Shaders, TronicShade styles, and Patchbooks (the black belt stuff)

### Shader import (BIN/ANS)
You can Import shader (BIN/ANS)… and/or Use shader BIN (curated FG/BG ramps).

You'll see status like:
- Shader: swatches=... pairs=... glyphPairs=... file=...

There's also:
- "Shader rows to read (top N rows)"
  Useful if your shader file has multiple rows of swatches and you only want the top chunk.

### Patchbook (learned style transfer)
Patchbooks are like a stash of learned glyph/color pair vibes.

You can:
- Load patchbook…
- Save patchbook…
- Clear patchbook

You may see:
- Patchbook: learned from import
- Or the app yelling: "Patchbook is empty. Import shader files first."

### Tronicshade styles
Tronicshade has its own style files:
- Load a TronicShade/Shader style profile from the styles folder
- Save as: *.tronic.json / *.tronic

It can also:
- Load ANSI into Tronicshade library (shade-only)

If you're chasing a consistent "house look" across a pack, Tronicshade styles + patchbooks are your repeatability weapons.

---

## Export options (BBS hygiene: SAUCE, iCE, line endings)

When saving ANSI, you'll typically have options like:

### Formatting / compatibility
- Trim trailing spaces (keeps lines from having pointless default cells)
- ClearScreenHome (adds the classic ESC[2J + ESC[H at the start)
- Line endings:
  - CRLF (Windows)
  - CR
  - LF

### SAUCE metadata (for the collectors and archivists)
- Add SAUCE metadata
- Title / Author / Group
- Optional source comment (writes a comment like SOURCE: <file>)
- Set iCE colors flag in SAUCE (bit0)
  (Use this if you intend iCE behavior and want viewers to know.)

### Convenience
- Copy source image beside ANSI file as *_source.<ext>
  Great for showing "before/after" in release zips.

---

## Common workflows (pick your mission)

### 1) Photo → ANSI poster
- Mode: hybrid
- Dither: moderate
- Contrast: 1.10–1.20
- Optional: ANSIrez median to reduce speckle

### 2) Pixel art → clean blocks
- ANSI Art Mode
- Try ansiblocks-pixel
- If colors are sacred: Glyph-only (keep existing colors)

### 3) "Make it look like that pack"
- Import shader BIN/ANS
- Let it learn patchbook
- Save patchbook + Tronicshade style
- Apply consistently across your set

---

## Troubleshooting (aka "why does it hate me?")

- "Open an image first."
  You didn't load an image.
- Render looks wrong / off-color
  Add Color Hints for the problem colors (logos/pastels).
- Too noisy
  Reduce dither strength, or enable ANSIrez (median/blend).
- Patchbook won't save / is empty
  Import shader files first so it has something to learn from.
- Need maximum compatibility
  Enable Clear screen + home, use CRLF, and add SAUCE.

---

## The vibe checklist ✅

Before you ship it to the BBS:

[ ] It's 80 columns wide (always)
[ ] You've previewed it in something real (PabloDraw/TheDraw/SyncTerm/etc.)
[ ] SAUCE is set (Title/Author/Group) for proper cred
[ ] If it uses iCE tricks, you set the iCE flag
[ ] You didn't accidentally Alt+R and nuke your perfect settings (RIP)

---
---

<a name="file-render-tab"></a>
# rez2ans — File & Render Tab 📁⚡

Alright, caller. This is the **File & Render** tab — the "get pixels in / get ANSI out" control deck.
If rez2ans is your BBS station, this tab is the **front panel switches**.

---

## What this tab is for
Use **File & Render** to:

- **Open** a source image (PNG/JPG/etc)
- **Pick a crop** by dragging a box on the *source preview*
- **Render Preview** (convert to 80xN cells)
- **Save** the result as **ANSI** (`.ans/.ansi`) or **BIN** (`.bin`)
- Manage **presets** (`.ini`) so you can reuse your perfect "house look" later

---

## Buttons row (top) — the "do stuff now" strip

### **Open Image…**
Loads an image into the left preview pane.

- Supported filter: PNG/JPG/JPEG/BMP (and whatever your system image loader can read)
- After opening, the status text will show:
  - `Loaded: <filename> | (not rendered yet)`

### **Render Preview**
Runs the conversion using your current settings and updates the output preview.
(Hotkey: **F12** also renders.)

If you haven't made a crop yet, rez2ans will nudge you with:
- `Open an image, drag a box to crop, then Render.`

### **Save…**
Exports the current render.

- Default extension is **.ans** (ANSI export is more portable than BIN)
- You can also save **.bin**
- If you choose ANSI, an export options dialog usually appears (SAUCE/iCE/line endings etc.)

Hotkey: **Alt+S**

### **Clear Selection**
Nukes your crop rectangle (sets selection back to none).

### **Preview Font…**
Lets you choose a custom font for the output preview **only**.
It does **not** change the conversion — just how the preview draws characters.

Important:
- If **BIOS preview** is enabled, rez2ans will refuse font picking and tell you to disable BIOS preview first.

### **BIOS preview**
When enabled, output preview uses **BIOS 8x16** style preview logic.

What it does in practice:
- Locks preview "cell width" to clean multiples of 8 pixels (so it stays crisp)
- Shows `Font: BIOS 8x16` in the font label
- Great for classic DOS look, less great if you're testing weird fonts

### **Load Preset…**
Load a saved preset file:
- `Preset (*.ini)`

### **Save Preset…**
Save your current UI/settings as a preset:
- `Preset (*.ini)`

Presets are how you keep "acid/hybrid/shader vibe" settings consistent across a whole set.

---

## Crop + sizing controls (left side of the tab)

### **Render selection only** (checkbox)
If enabled (default), rez2ans renders **only the crop box** you dragged on the source preview.

- Checked + you have a valid selection → crop is used
- Unchecked (or no selection) → rez2ans renders from the full image area (no crop)

### **Rows (0=auto)** (spinbox)
Forces the output height in text rows.

- `0` = **auto** (rez2ans decides the number of rows based on your crop + settings)
- `>0` = **forced** number of rows (useful if you're targeting a fixed layout)

Think of this as: "how tall is my final ANSI?"

### **CellW (pixels)** (spinbox)
Controls the **preview scale** (not the conversion).

It's literally "how wide should each character cell be in the output preview window?"

- Normal preview: minimum ~4 px
- BIOS preview: minimum 8 px and snaps to 8px steps
- Cell height is drawn as ~2x CellW (keeps DOS-ish aspect)

If the output preview looks tiny or huge, this is your zoom knob.

---

## The two info readouts (status text for humans)

### **Info line**
Shows load/render status like:
- `Loaded: FILE.PNG | Output: 80 x 123 | BIN bytes: 19680`
or
- `Loaded: FILE.PNG | (not rendered yet)`

### **Selection line**
Shows crop box coordinates and size, like:
- `Selection: (x,y) - (x,y) size=WxH px`
or
- `Selection: (none)`

---

## Where the actual cropping happens (source preview)
The crop box is created by dragging on the **source preview pane** (left big image area):

1) **Left-click + drag** on the source preview to draw a rectangle
2) Release to set the selection
3) Render Preview (or F12)

Tiny drags (basically "clicks") are treated as no selection.

Pro tip:
- Right-click can be used in "hint dropper" mode (when enabled elsewhere) to exit that mode, but for normal cropping, just drag with left mouse.

---

## Output preview pane (right side)
After rendering, the right preview shows the 80xN output using CP437 glyphs and ANSI 16-color preview.

- It's scrollable if your ANSI is tall
- Clicking the output preview may jump you into tile/glyph inspection features (depending on mode)

---

## Hotkeys (also shown in the bottom status bar)
- **F12** — Render
- **Alt+S** — Save (ANSI/BIN)
- **Alt+R** — Reset defaults (big red "oops" button)

---

## Typical workflow (the "one-two punch")
1) **Open Image…**
2) Drag a crop box on the source preview
3) (Optional) set **Rows** if you need a fixed height
4) **Render Preview** (or **F12**)
5) Adjust preview zoom with **CellW (pixels)**
6) **Save…** (or **Alt+S**)
7) Save a **Preset** if you want repeatable pack vibes

---

If you want, I can also write a tiny "quick cheatsheet" version of this tab (like a 30-line NFO-style quick ref).

---
---

<a name="image-adjustments-tab"></a>
# rez2ans — Image Adjustments Tab 🎛️🧪

Welcome to the **Image Adjustments** tab — the place where you **tune the source image** before rez2ans turns it into CP437 glory.
Think of it as your **pre-flight color lab**: you're not changing the final ANSI file directly… you're changing what the renderer *sees*.

If your render looks:
- washed out
- too dark
- muddy
- neon-radioactive
- "why is everything GREEN??"

…this tab is where you fix it.

---

## What this tab affects (important)
These sliders typically affect the **input image processing stage**:

- They change the **working image** used for palette matching + glyph selection
- They do **not** edit your original file on disk
- They do **not** "draw" anything into the ANSI — they influence the renderer's decisions

Rule of thumb:
> Adjustments first → then Render (F12).

---

## The core knobs (the "four horsemen")

### 1) Brightness
Moves everything up/down.

- Too dark render? bump brightness slightly.
- Too bright / blown highlights? reduce a bit.

**Good starting zone:** ~0.90–1.10

Scene tip:
- Brightness is your "global lift." Use it gently so you don't nuke contrast.

---

### 2) Gamma
Changes midtones without smashing blacks/whites as hard as brightness.

- If the render feels "flat" or "muddy," tweak gamma.
- If shadows are crushed but highlights are fine, gamma is often the move.

**Good starting zone:** ~0.95–1.05

Scene tip:
- Gamma is the secret "it suddenly reads better" slider.
- Micro moves matter. Don't go full DJ-on-the-bass-knob.

---

### 3) Contrast
Expands/compresses differences.

- If output looks like fog → increase contrast.
- If it looks like harsh posterization → decrease contrast.

**Good starting zone:** ~1.10–1.25

Scene tip:
- Contrast helps the glyph solver find edges and structure,
  but too much contrast can force ugly color jumps in ANSI-16 land.

---

### 4) Saturation
Color intensity.

- If it looks gray and sad → increase saturation.
- If it looks like a laser tag arena → decrease saturation.

**Good starting zone:** ~0.90–1.10

Scene tip:
- ANSI has only 16 colors (plus bright variants). Over-saturation can make palette matching freak out.
- If colors are "wrong," try *lowering* saturation before doing anything fancy.

---

## Dither / diffusion controls (the "texture engine")
Depending on your mode (hires/hybrid/shades/tronicshade/etc), you'll often see controls like:

### Dither amount / strength
How much "pattern" is introduced to simulate missing colors/shades.

- Low: cleaner, but can band
- High: more texture, but can sparkle/noise

Use it when:
- Smooth gradients band (increase dither)
- The output looks like pepper spray (decrease dither)

### Ordered dither (Bayer)
Some modes use ordered patterns (Bayer4/Bayer8). Ordered dither looks more "grid intentional" than random noise.

- Bayer4: more visible classic ANSI texture
- Bayer8: smoother/less obvious

---

## How to use adjustments (fast workflow)
1) Open image
2) Drag crop
3) Render once (F12) to see the baseline
4) Adjust ONE knob at a time
5) Render again (F12)
6) When it's close, use tiny tweaks (0.02 steps feel huge)

Pro move:
- Make a preset once you find a "house look" that works for your typical sources.

---

## Common problems + fixes (the "why does it look like that??" guide)

### "Everything is too dark / crushed"
Try:
- Brightness up a touch (1.05)
- Gamma up slightly (1.02)
- Contrast down if blacks are brutal

### "Everything is washed out / no punch"
Try:
- Contrast up (1.15–1.25)
- Gamma down slightly (0.98–0.95) if midtones are too airy

### "Colors are wrong / weird ANSI swaps"
Try:
- Saturation down (0.90–0.95)
- Then use **Color Hints** (in the hints area) for problem colors (logos, skin tones)

### "Too speckly / noisy"
Try:
- Dither amount down
- Or use ANSIrez / TronicShade settle passes (depending on your mode)

### "Banding in gradients"
Try:
- Increase dither amount a bit
- If you have Bayer options, try Bayer8 for smoother texture

---

## Suggested starting presets (quick cheats)
These are safe "first try" values:

### Photo-ish sources (hybrid/tronicshade)
- Brightness: 1.00
- Gamma: 1.00
- Contrast: 1.15
- Saturation: 1.00
- Dither: moderate (don't overcook)

### Line art / flat graphics
- Brightness: 1.00
- Gamma: 1.00
- Contrast: 1.20–1.30
- Saturation: 0.95–1.05
- Dither: low-to-medium

### Pastel-heavy images (easy to ruin in ANSI-16)
- Brightness: 1.00
- Gamma: 1.02
- Contrast: 1.10–1.15
- Saturation: 0.90–0.98
- Then add Color Hints for key pastel tones

---

## Hotkeys / reminders
- Render after changes: **F12**
- Save output: **Alt+S**
- Full reset to defaults (careful!): **Alt+R**

---

If you want, tell me what kind of images you're feeding rez2ans (photos / pixel art / logos / scans)
and I'll write you a mini "starter preset" for Image Adjustments + your preferred render mode.

---
---

<a name="ansi-art-tab"></a>
# rez2ans — ANSI Art Tab 🖋️🧱  (Glyphfit + Autoshade ONLY)

Yo. This tab is **ANSI Art** — but here's the important catch:

> ✅ **ANSI Art is ONLY used by the `glyphfit` and `autoshade` modes.**  
> If you're in **hires / shades / hybrid / tronicshade** and you're poking this tab like "why nothing happen?" …that's why. 😄

Think of ANSI Art as the **glyph brain + rulebook** that those two modes consult while they're picking characters.

---

## What ANSI Art does (in plain BBS)
ANSI Art controls:

- **Which glyph sets are allowed** (blocks? shades? halfblocks? weird CP437 toys?)
- **How hard it prefers certain glyph families** (clean blocks vs gritty shading)
- **Whether it's allowed to use "glyph-only" approaches**
- **How it interprets an imported ANSI** (for re-char / style anchoring)

In other words:
> Image comes in → `glyphfit`/`autoshade` ask ANSI Art:  
> "What characters am I allowed to use, and what do you want me to favor?"

---

# 1) Mode dependency (don't fight the UI)
### Works with:
- ✅ **glyphfit**
- ✅ **autoshade**

### Not used by:
- ❌ hires
- ❌ shades
- ❌ hybrid
- ❌ tronicshade (has its own engine + style files)

So: **pick glyphfit/autoshade first**, then come here.

---

# 2) ANSI Art Modes (typical options you'll see)

## A) ansiblocks
Classic block/halfblock-driven ANSI building blocks.
Good for:
- clean shapes
- logos
- chunky "scene pack" look

## B) ansiblocks-pixel
More pixel-art minded.
Good for:
- pixel sprites
- crisp edges
- avoiding "letter soup"

## C) Load ANSI… (import existing .ans/.ansi)
This lets you bring in a real ANSI file as a reference.
What that can do:
- bias glyph selection toward what the imported ANSI uses
- enable "re-char" workflows (depending on options below)

This is your:
> "Make mine feel like THIS pack" button (especially for glyphfit/autoshade).

---

# 3) Glyph-only options (aka "re-char without repaint")

You may see options like:

## Glyph-only (characters only)
The renderer focuses on picking characters; color handling becomes simpler or separate.

## Glyph-only (keep existing colors; change characters only)
This one is **gold** if you already have an ANSI with colors you like, and you want to:
- keep FG/BG attributes
- swap/upgrade characters to improve shading/detail

Scene usage:
- "My palette is perfect, but my glyphs are potato." → Use this.

Important:
- This is most meaningful when you've **loaded an ANSI** as a base/reference.

---

# 4) Glyph set selection (the "allowed alphabet")
ANSI Art usually lets you choose (or implies) a glyph set. Common families:

### Blocks
- █ full block, and friends
- makes output bold and clean

### Shades
- ░▒▓ (and █)
- makes output "textured" but controlled

### Half-blocks / partial blocks
- ▀▄▌▐ and similar
- gives smoother edges + more pseudo-resolution

### Extended CP437 glyphs
- can add detail, but also risk "glyph salad" if overused

**Rule of thumb:**
- Want **clean** → blocks + limited shades
- Want **grit** → shades + halfblocks
- Want **pixel** → ansiblocks-pixel
- Want **max detail** → glyphfit + broader glyph set (careful)

---

# 5) How glyphfit uses ANSI Art (why it matters)
`glyphfit` is the "shape matching" mode:
- It tries to choose characters whose **pixel pattern** matches the source structure in each cell.
- ANSI Art decides which patterns are on the menu.

If you allow tons of glyphs:
- it can match detail better
- but can get noisy / inconsistent

If you constrain to blocks/shades:
- it looks cleaner and more "ANSI scene"
- but you lose micro-detail

So ANSI Art is basically your:
> "Clean vs detail" lever for glyphfit.

---

# 6) How autoshade uses ANSI Art (why it matters)
`autoshade` is the "stylize with shading rules" mode:
- It leans into shade/block logic and tries to make lighting/texture coherent.
- ANSI Art determines the shading vocabulary (░▒▓█ vs halfblocks etc.).

If autoshade output looks:
- too grainy → reduce allowed glyph variety (favor shades over random CP437)
- too blocky → allow more halfblocks, or reduce strict block preference
- too flat → allow more shade levels (░▒▓), raise diffusion/dither a bit

---

# 7) Practical recipes (fast wins)

## Recipe: Clean logo / poster
- Mode: **autoshade** or **glyphfit**
- ANSI Art: **ansiblocks**
- Limit glyphs to: blocks + small shade set
- Dither/diffusion: low
Result: bold, clean, pack-friendly.

## Recipe: Pixel art sprite
- Mode: **glyphfit**
- ANSI Art: **ansiblocks-pixel**
- Keep glyph set tight (avoid fancy CP437)
- Dither: low-to-medium
Result: crisp edges, less weird glyph noise.

## Recipe: Re-char an existing ANSI (keep colors)
- Mode: **glyphfit**
- Load ANSI…
- Enable **Glyph-only (keep existing colors)**
- Render + tweak glyph constraints
Result: palette stays; characters get upgraded.

---

# 8) Gotchas (aka "why is it acting weird?")

### "ANSI Art tab does nothing"
You're not in **glyphfit/autoshade**.

### "Output is full of weird symbols"
Your glyph set is too broad. Tighten it to blocks/shades.

### "Looks noisy / shimmering"
Reduce dither/diffusion and/or restrict glyph variety.
(Then render again.)

### "Not matching the imported ANSI vibe"
Try:
- loading a different ANSI reference (same "crew style")
- using glyph-only options
- tightening glyph sets to match the reference's vocabulary

---

# Hotkeys reminder
- Render: **F12**
- Save: **Alt+S**
- Reset defaults: **Alt+R** (careful, it wipes your magic sauce)

---

If you want, tell me what you're aiming for (toon clean / gritty / pixel),
and I'll write a **two-minute ANSI Art setup**: which mode, which glyph set, and which toggles to flip.

---
---

<a name="patchstyle-tab"></a>
# rez2ans — PatchStyle Tab 🧩🎨  (a.k.a. "Patchbook Style Transfer")

PatchStyle is where rez2ans stops being "just a converter" and starts doing **style theft** (the good kind).
It lets you **apply a learned patchbook** (tiny chunks of real ANSI/BIN) onto your render so it comes out
more like a **pack look** instead of "generic image-to-ANSI."

**In-universe:** you feed it a reference style (via shader/ANSI/BIN imports), it learns a stash of patterns,
then it *stamps similar patterns* onto your new art.

---

## What PatchStyle actually does
PatchStyle takes your already-rendered cell grid (80xN) and runs a **multi-scale patch matching pass**:

- It breaks the screen into overlapping **patch windows** (in *cell space*).
- For each window it computes a simple **signature** of the region (rough tone/luma pattern).
- It searches the patchbook for the **closest signature match**.
- If the match is good enough, it **applies the patch**:
  - either **Full** (glyphs + colors)
  - or **Glyph-only** (glyphs only, keep your colors)

Patch sizes are typically:
- **10x10** (big structure)
- **5x5** (mid structure)
- **3x3** (micro texture)

And it can repeat the cycle: **10 → 5 → 3** for multiple loops.

Scene translation:
> 10 fixes the big shapes, 5 tightens it up, 3 sprinkles the texture.

---

## IMPORTANT: Where PatchStyle is used
PatchStyle is designed for the **GlyphFit / AutoShade pipeline**.
So it's most meaningful when your conversion mode is:
- ✅ **glyphfit**
- ✅ **autoshade**

If you're living in **tronicshade**, PatchStyle is usually not the main course (TronicShade has its own library/passes).
Still, PatchStyle is its own thing: it needs a patchbook to do anything.

---

# Patchbook 101 — where do patches come from?
PatchStyle needs a **patchbook** (learned patches). You get it by:

### A) Importing style reference files (recommended)
Import shader/ANSI/BIN reference files in the shader tooling.
When you import, rez2ans can **learn patches from the imported grid** automatically.

If Patchbook is empty, rez2ans will basically tell you:
> "Patchbook is empty. Import shader files first."

### B) Loading a saved patchbook
If you already have one, use:
- **Load Patchbook…**

### C) Saving your current patchbook
Once you've got a good one:
- **Save Patchbook…**
so you can reuse it across releases.

### D) Clearing it (start fresh)
- **Clear Patchbook**
Wipes it back to empty.

---

# PatchStyle controls (what the knobs mean)

## 1) Enable PatchStyle
**PatchStyle Enabled** (checkbox)

- OFF: patchbook is ignored (no style transfer)
- ON: patch matching runs after the initial render

If this is ON but your patchbook is empty, nothing happens.

---

## 2) Patch sizes (10 / 5 / 3)
These checkboxes decide which patch scales are allowed:

### ✅ Use 10x10
- Big "macro" chunks (strong style identity)
- Can enforce chunky shapes / big shading regions
- Heavier pass (more noticeable influence)

### ✅ Use 5x5
- Mid-scale structure
- Often the "sweet spot" for making things feel like the reference pack

### ✅ Use 3x3
- Micro texture / stipple / shading flavor
- Great for consistent grain, can also introduce noise if overused

**Recommended defaults:**
- Start with **5 + 3**
- Add **10** if you want the style to hit harder

---

## 3) Loops (how many times it runs)
**Loops** = number of **10 → 5 → 3 cycles**.

- 1 loop: subtle, safer
- 2–3 loops: stronger "pack look"
- 4+ loops: can overcook / start bulldozing detail

Rule of thumb:
> If it looks "too forced," reduce loops.

---

## 4) Min Match % (signature threshold)
**Min Match %** controls how strict PatchStyle is about applying a patch.

- Higher % = only apply when it's very similar (safer, fewer wrong stamps)
- Lower % = apply more often (stronger style transfer, more risk)

Think of it as:
> "How confident do we need to be before we slap a patch down?"

Suggested ranges:
- **80–90%**: conservative, clean
- **70–80%**: medium aggression (often good)
- **<70%**: spicy — can get "wrong texture in wrong places"

---

## 5) Apply Mode (what gets stamped)
Usually a radio group like:

### **Full (glyph + colors)**
PatchStyle overwrites:
- glyphs (characters)
- attributes (FG/BG colors)

This is the most intense style transfer.
Great when you want the reference pack's *color behavior* too.

Risk:
- can shift colors away from your intended palette match

### **Glyph-only**
PatchStyle overwrites:
- glyphs only

Your colors stay as rendered (from your current conversion settings).
Great when you like your colors, but want the reference *texture/glyph vocabulary*.

This is the "safer scene" mode.

---

## 6) Blocks/Shades only (the "no punctuation" filter)
**Blocks/Shades only** keeps the patchbook restricted to:
- space
- ░ ▒ ▓ █  (CP437 176/177/178/219)

Why you'd do this:
- you want PatchStyle to guide *shading ramps* only
- you do NOT want it importing random texture glyphs / punctuation into your output

This is super good for:
- clean pack looks
- avoiding "glyph salad"
- keeping PatchStyle from turning faces into keyboard vomit

Note:
- If you load a patchbook and this is enabled, rez2ans can filter it immediately.

---

# The patch info label (what it's telling you)
You'll typically see status like:
- `Patchbook: (empty)`
- `Patchbook: loaded`
- `Patchbook: loaded (blocks/shades only)`

If it's empty, PatchStyle has no ammo.

---

# Practical recipes (dial sheets)

## Recipe: "Make it look like the reference pack" (strong transfer)
- Apply Mode: **Full**
- Use: **10 + 5 + 3**
- Loops: **2**
- Min Match: **75–85**
- Blocks-only: OFF (unless the reference uses mostly shades/blocks)

## Recipe: "Keep my colors, steal the texture" (safe + clean)
- Apply Mode: **Glyph-only**
- Use: **5 + 3**
- Loops: **1–2**
- Min Match: **80–90**
- Blocks-only: ON (often)

## Recipe: "Just smooth shading ramps" (no weird glyphs)
- Apply Mode: **Glyph-only**
- Use: **3 only** (or 5+3)
- Loops: **1**
- Min Match: **85–95**
- Blocks-only: ON

---

# Troubleshooting (aka "why is it not doing anything?")

### "PatchStyle enabled but nothing changes"
- Patchbook is empty → import shader/ANSI/BIN references first, or load a patchbook file.

### "It's stamping wrong textures in weird spots"
- Raise **Min Match %**
- Reduce **Loops**
- Try **Glyph-only**
- Consider **Blocks-only** to prevent punctuation/texture glyphs

### "Colors got wrecked"
- You're probably in **Full** mode → switch to **Glyph-only**
- Or tighten Min Match so fewer patches overwrite attrs

### "It got too same-y / overfitted"
- Reduce Loops
- Disable 10x10 (macro stamping can bulldoze variety)

---

# Hotkeys reminder
- Render: **F12**
- Save: **Alt+S**
- Reset defaults: **Alt+R** (careful—wipes your sauce)

---

If you tell me what style you're chasing (toon clean / gritty halfblocks / shade-ramp smooth),
I can suggest a PatchStyle setup that hits it without trashing detail.

---
---

<a name="tronicshade-ansilab"></a>
# rez2ans — TronicShade + AnsiLab Deep Dive 😈💾

This doc goes deeper on rez2ans's two "secret sauce" systems:

- TronicShade: the solver + style engine that makes output feel like real ANSI.
- AnsiLab: the trainer that learns a pack's "house look" and writes presets.

---

## TronicShade — the "make it look like real ANSI" engine

### What TronicShade is (in rez2ans terms)
TronicShade is a rendering pipeline that outputs 80xN ANSI/BIN by:

1) Sampling the source image at glyph-cell resolution (8x16-ish).
2) Picking FG/BG colors (ANSI 16 palette, with candidate guidance).
3) Picking a glyph biased toward shading + blocks (░▒▓█ plus half-blocks ▀▄ etc.).
4) Running post-passes that reduce noise, preserve edges, and make shading flow.

So it's not just "nearest ASCII." It's:
image → cell grid → glyph+attr solve → style passes → output.

---

### The TronicShade "library" (style files)
TronicShade can load style profiles (often *.tronic.json) that contain a library of swatches.

Think of a swatch as:
"If a cell's pixel-pattern looks like this, then this FG/BG + this shading mask is a good move."

Example fields commonly found in a style:
- blockW / blockH: neighborhood sizing for matching.
- swatches: count of learned samples.
- each swatch often has:
  - fg / bg (0–15)
  - bits: a 64-bit 0/1 mask (effectively an 8x8 pattern)
  - stamp: a weight/score value

Style profiles also include vibe parameters like:
- blockStrength: how hard it leans into chunky block structure
- edgeKeep: how much it protects edges from being smeared
- verticalSmear: allowance for vertical blending

Translation: the style file is a taste profile. Load a different one and the same image can render with a different crew aesthetic.

---

### The big TronicShade knobs (what they really do)

#### 1) Strength (how hard we force the vibe)
- Low: more literal, less stylized
- High: more "it's going to look like ANSI no matter what"

Crank it for:
- more deliberate blocks
- less photo-real mush
- stronger identity

#### 2) Ordered diffusion (Bayer4/Bayer8) + Diffusion Amount
TronicShade uses ordered diffusion as a controlled luma offset.

- Bayer4: more visible grid texture (classic ANSI dither vibe)
- Bayer8: smoother, less blocky pattern

Diffusion Amount is how much that ordered texture influences picking.
Use it to reduce banding, but don't overcook the pattern.

#### 3) AutoShader windowing (Window Size / Step)
Builds a smoothed "tone target field" (lighting guide) that later passes use to keep texture coherent.

Why you care:
texture spans and reads like lighting, instead of confetti noise.

#### 4) Histogram-guided palette candidates (Top N)
Rather than considering every palette choice equally, it can use local histogram info to pick Top N likely candidates.

Effect:
- more stable choices
- fewer "why THAT color??" moments

---

## TronicShade post-passes (where the magic gets finished)

### A) Span Shade pass (texture that flows)
This pass estimates local light direction from the tone field and nudges shading to follow it.

- Skips strong-gradient areas (keeps sharp edges crisp)
- Acts in solid-ish regions (where neighbors share attributes)
- Uses an ordered threshold (Bayer) to vary shade levels nicely

Result:
shading looks like it follows form/light instead of static stipple.

### B) Edge Shade pass
Converts leftover half-block seams into shade texture where helpful, smoothing awkward boundaries.

### C) Settle / Refine pass (the coherence loop)
Heavy cleanup pass that tries to reduce shimmer and speckle while protecting edges.

What it tends to do:
- prefers a shade/block glyph set (space, ░▒▓█, half-blocks)
- samples the source inside each cell (8x16) to compare decisions
- uses coherence forces: BG stability, attr stability, run continuity, "stickiness"

Iteration count often increases with higher TronicShadeWeight.

---

## TronicShadeWeight (the sleeper MVP)
This roughly controls how much settle/refine happens.

- Low: raw solver texture, more noise/sparkle
- High: stronger coherence, smoother and more "finished"

If output is:
- too noisy → raise ShadeWeight
- too smeary → lower ShadeWeight (or increase edgeKeep via style)

---

## Practical "scene recipes" for TronicShade

### TOON / posterized pack look
- Strength: ~120–150
- Diffusion Amount: ~40–55
- ShadeWeight: ~80–110
- BlockThreshold: higher (more willingness to place █ cleanly)

### DEATH / gritty / textured
- Strength: ~180–200
- Diffusion Amount: ~60–75
- ShadeWeight: ~130–160
- BlockThreshold: lower (more half-block/shade structure)

---

# AnsiLab — teach rez2ans what your favorite ANSI tastes like

## What AnsiLab does
AnsiLab is a training / preset builder.

You feed it one or more ANSI files (or imports) and it can:
1) Import them into TronicShade's library (learn patterns).
2) Import them into the shader system (better priors).
3) Compute training stats: how many cells are space, █, ░▒▓, half-block directions, etc.
4) Save outputs:
   - a Tronic style file (learned vibe)
   - a shader profile
   - an AnsiLab manifest (*.ansilab.json)
   - a preset (*.preset.ini) that sets recommended defaults

In English:
"I want it to come out like that pack" → AnsiLab writes a preset that makes it do that.

---

## AnsiLab options (what they mean)

### Treat blink as iCE (or bright BG)
Decides how AnsiLab interprets blink in training files so it doesn't learn illegal or unintended BG choices.

### MirrorH
Learns mirrored patterns too (helps symmetry and reduces directional bias).

### Learn shade-only
Very important: learns only shade/block glyphs (avoids "training on text letters").

### MaxRows
Limit how deep into tall files it learns.

### Passes
Multiple directional passes so learning isn't biased to one scan direction.

### Weight + DedupeCap
- Weight: contribution strength per file
- DedupeCap: prevents one repeated patch from dominating the library

---

## The fun part: AnsiLab's auto-recommended settings
AnsiLab computes rough pack DNA from ratios like:
- shadePct = % of ░▒▓
- fullPct = % of █
- halfPct = % of half-block directions

Then it can recommend TronicShade defaults:

### "Toon-ish" heuristic
If shadePct < 1% AND fullPct > 45%:
- Strength ~130
- Diffusion ~45
- ShadeWeight ~90
- BlockThreshold ~65

### "Death-ish / gritty" heuristic
If shadePct >= 2.5% AND halfPct >= 25%:
- Strength ~200
- Diffusion ~70
- ShadeWeight ~140
- BlockThreshold ~48

### Otherwise (middle/default)
- Strength ~170
- Diffusion ~60
- ShadeWeight ~120
- BlockThreshold ~55

So AnsiLab is literally reading the pack DNA and dialing TronicShade accordingly.

---

## Suggested workflow
1) Open AnsiLab tab
2) Name preset (e.g., "toonpack_clean" or "gritdeath_halfblocks")
3) Usually enable: Shade-only, MirrorH
4) Decide blink interpretation (depends on training ANSI)
5) Set passes (3 is a good start) and dedupe cap (4 is sane)
6) Build
7) Apply the generated preset and render your images through it

Result: consistent "house style" output — perfect for packs/sets.

---
---

